WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.190
A FundsXML file has arrived.

2
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:08.903
How does it get into the database, safely, even when it is large, and even when the same file arrives twice?

3
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:18.910
In this film you see the steps of a robust loader, the traps on the way, and the loader of the complete example at work, on six deliveries and four broken files.

4
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.588
You can download all of it.

5
00:00:21.838 --> 00:00:28.028
A robust loader works in six steps, and the example follows them for every file in its landing folder.

6
00:00:28.078 --> 00:00:31.454
Check the file: is it complete, and from a known sender?

7
00:00:31.504 --> 00:00:33.350
Validate it against the schema.

8
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.917
Register the delivery with its document ID; a document ID that is already known is skipped.

9
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:42.649
Read the data and write it to the tables, in one transaction.

10
00:00:42.699 --> 00:00:45.524
Reconcile the result, and only then release it.

11
00:00:45.574 --> 00:00:50.296
A file that fails a check moves to the error folder, with a report next to it.

12
00:00:50.346 --> 00:00:54.945
The original file is never changed, and every step writes a line to the log.

13
00:00:56.295 --> 00:00:57.817
The first check is cheap.

14
00:00:57.867 --> 00:00:59.138
Is the file complete?

15
00:00:59.188 --> 00:01:06.809
A file cut off during transfer has no closing root element; the example reads only the last bytes of the file to find out.

16
00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:09.136
Does it come from an expected sender?

17
00:01:09.186 --> 00:01:14.544
The control data at the top of the file says so, and which schema version it declares.

18
00:01:14.594 --> 00:01:16.556
Zipped files are unpacked first.

19
00:01:16.606 --> 00:01:19.071
Only then does the expensive work begin.

20
00:01:20.421 --> 00:01:21.990
When does the loader start?

21
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:27.626
Either a watcher notices every new file and starts at once, or the loader runs at fixed times.

22
00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:34.401
The best of both: load as soon as a file arrives, and raise an alarm when a file has not arrived by its deadline.

23
00:01:34.451 --> 00:01:39.917
Then a late delivery is noticed in the morning, not at noon, when someone asks for the data.

24
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.978
Validate first, load later.

25
00:01:43.028 --> 00:01:50.215
A file that fails the schema is not loaded at all, not even partly, because half a delivery is worse than none.

26
00:01:50.265 --> 00:01:54.323
The error report is kept with the file, and the sender is told.

27
00:01:54.373 --> 00:01:59.996
Remember that FundsXML needs an X S D one point one validator, such as Saxon or Xerces.

28
00:02:01.346 --> 00:02:03.326
Which schema to validate against?

29
00:02:03.376 --> 00:02:07.140
Against the version the file declares, as long as you know it.

30
00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:14.398
When a partner already sends a newer version, validate against that one, load the elements you know, and log the rest.

31
00:02:14.448 --> 00:02:20.180
The original file is in the archive, so nothing is lost; the new elements can be loaded later.

32
00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:26.867
Files arrive twice more often than you think: a repeated transfer, a manual upload.

33
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:32.449
So the loader registers every document ID, together with a hash and the original file.

34
00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:35.434
If the same ID comes again, it skips the file.

35
00:02:35.484 --> 00:02:40.432
If a delivery is a correction, it replaces the data of the delivery it names.

36
00:02:40.482 --> 00:02:44.391
That way, loading the same file twice never doubles the data.

37
00:02:45.741 --> 00:02:51.951
Another question to settle with each partner: does a delivery contain the full state, or only the changes?

38
00:02:52.001 --> 00:02:56.558
A full delivery replaces everything the receiver knows for that fund and date.

39
00:02:56.608 --> 00:03:00.515
A delta delivery only adds and changes, and needs a complete start.

40
00:03:00.565 --> 00:03:04.294
Full deliveries are simpler and more robust; deltas save volume.

41
00:03:04.344 --> 00:03:07.011
Your loader must know which one it is reading.

42
00:03:08.361 --> 00:03:09.654
How to read the file?

43
00:03:09.704 --> 00:03:13.106
The simplest way loads the whole document into memory.

44
00:03:13.156 --> 00:03:15.984
That is fine for prices and small portfolios.

45
00:03:16.034 --> 00:03:23.656
But a delivery with thousands of positions, regulatory templates and embedded documents can reach hundreds of megabytes.

46
00:03:23.706 --> 00:03:29.283
Then read it as a stream, element by element: with StAX in Java, or iterparse in Python.

47
00:03:30.633 --> 00:03:32.171
Streaming has one catch.

48
00:03:32.221 --> 00:03:37.266
In FundsXML, the asset master data comes after the funds and their positions.

49
00:03:37.316 --> 00:03:40.972
So while reading a position, its asset is not known yet.

50
00:03:41.022 --> 00:03:44.214
Two solutions: read the file twice, assets first.

51
00:03:44.264 --> 00:03:49.574
Or write positions into a staging table and link them to their assets at the end.

52
00:03:49.624 --> 00:03:51.890
The example loader does the second.

53
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.544
This is the core of the example loader.

54
00:03:55.594 --> 00:04:06.226
It walks through the file once and calls one handler for every repeating element: a fund, a subfund, a share class, a position, a trade, an asset, a document, a template record.

55
00:04:06.276 --> 00:04:10.511
Funds and subfunds have the same parts, so one name covers both levels.

56
00:04:10.561 --> 00:04:17.391
After each element, its memory is released, so neither the size of the file nor the number of funds in it matters.

57
00:04:18.741 --> 00:04:22.472
Several funds in one file are no special case for such a loader.

58
00:04:22.522 --> 00:04:27.080
It keeps a small stack: the umbrella, and inside it the subfund it is reading.

59
00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:35.291
A share class arrives with all its prices, net assets, flows and distributions, and every price becomes a row with its date and its nature.

60
00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:41.789
The month-end file of the example, with five funds and more than a thousand records, goes through in one pass.

61
00:04:43.139 --> 00:04:43.964
Then the keys.

62
00:04:44.014 --> 00:04:51.838
For each asset, the loader looks for it by its I S I N, or by the key built from sender, type and the sender's own identifier.

63
00:04:51.888 --> 00:04:53.838
If it is unknown, it creates it.

64
00:04:53.888 --> 00:04:57.900
If it is known, it updates its master data with the newer values.

65
00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:05.024
New assets are counted in the log, because a sudden flood of new assets often points to a problem in the delivery.

66
00:05:06.374 --> 00:05:08.782
The other parts follow the same pattern.

67
00:05:08.832 --> 00:05:17.506
Trades and income also refer to assets, so they wait until the asset master data has been read; there are few of them, so they wait in memory.

68
00:05:17.556 --> 00:05:24.141
Embedded documents are decoded, their size is compared with the size the file declares, and they get a hash.

69
00:05:24.191 --> 00:05:28.749
And every template record is kept as X M L, with its key fields as columns.

70
00:05:30.099 --> 00:05:32.829
Here are the three templates of the example.

71
00:05:32.879 --> 00:05:48.373
Each handler names the template and its version, the share class or portfolio it reports on, and the few fields that are kept as columns: the ongoing costs of the E M T, the S F D R product type of the E E T, the number of positions of the T P T.

72
00:05:48.423 --> 00:05:52.290
A new template version needs a new line here, not a new table.

73
00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.352
Writing happens in one transaction per file.

74
00:05:56.402 --> 00:06:01.436
If anything goes wrong, everything is rolled back, and the database is as before.

75
00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:04.700
For speed, rows are written in bulk, not one by one.

76
00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:12.671
And after the cause of an error has been fixed, the file is simply loaded again; thanks to the document ID, nothing is doubled.

77
00:06:14.021 --> 00:06:16.879
Not every problem is a reason to reject a file.

78
00:06:16.929 --> 00:06:18.426
A schema error always is.

79
00:06:18.476 --> 00:06:25.419
But a business warning, such as an unusually large price change, may be correct; load the data, flag it, and ask.

80
00:06:25.469 --> 00:06:33.896
A real business error, such as weights that add up to a hundred and twenty percent, holds the delivery back until the sender corrects it.

81
00:06:33.946 --> 00:06:36.371
Agree on these rules with your partners.

82
00:06:37.721 --> 00:06:38.882
How fast can it be?

83
00:06:38.932 --> 00:06:45.509
With bulk inserts, or the copy command of PostgreSQL, a loader writes thousands of positions per second.

84
00:06:45.559 --> 00:06:49.843
Large tables load faster when some indexes are built after the load.

85
00:06:49.893 --> 00:06:55.450
And processing one fund at a time keeps memory small and lets you restart in the middle.

86
00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.573
Speed is rarely the real problem; correctness is.

87
00:06:59.923 --> 00:07:03.006
Here the example loader runs over the landing folder.

88
00:07:03.056 --> 00:07:14.948
The month-end file: checked, valid, registered as delivery one, then five funds, eleven share classes, five hundred twenty-two positions, two hundred nine trades and twelve template records in one pass.

89
00:07:14.998 --> 00:07:22.160
Every position is linked to its asset, the codes are checked, the four portfolios reconcile, and the delivery is released.

90
00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:24.643
Then the daily prices, then the documents.

91
00:07:25.993 --> 00:07:27.036
Then a correction.

92
00:07:27.086 --> 00:07:38.455
The amend names the daily file it corrects; the loader marks all sixty-six prices and one hundred fourteen net asset values of that file as replaced, and loads the complete corrected data.

93
00:07:38.505 --> 00:07:44.893
A file sent by mistake is taken back by a delete: its rows are marked replaced, and nothing new is loaded.

94
00:07:44.943 --> 00:07:47.869
Nothing is overwritten; both stay in the history.

95
00:07:49.219 --> 00:07:50.763
And the four broken files.

96
00:07:50.813 --> 00:07:55.545
The file cut off during transfer fails the cheap check, before any validation.

97
00:07:55.595 --> 00:08:00.388
The second copy of the daily file is skipped, because its document ID is known.

98
00:08:00.438 --> 00:08:03.085
The file from an unknown sender is rejected.

99
00:08:03.135 --> 00:08:07.192
And the file with two elements in the wrong order fails the schema.

100
00:08:07.242 --> 00:08:12.403
Six loaded, one skipped, three rejected, and the database is exactly as it should be.

101
00:08:13.753 --> 00:08:15.684
And what is in the database now?

102
00:08:15.734 --> 00:08:16.736
A look with psql.

103
00:08:16.786 --> 00:08:19.769
Six deliveries, five funds, eleven share classes.

104
00:08:19.819 --> 00:08:30.539
Two hundred eighty-five net asset values and four hundred eighteen prices: that includes the rows replaced by the correction and the deletion, because nothing is overwritten.

105
00:08:30.589 --> 00:08:39.948
Four portfolios with five hundred twenty-two positions, four hundred thirty assets, two hundred nine trades, nine documents and twelve template records.

106
00:08:39.998 --> 00:08:41.743
Every number matches the log.

107
00:08:43.093 --> 00:08:45.500
Before the data is released, reconcile it.

108
00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.659
Do the position values add up to the total net assets?

109
00:08:48.709 --> 00:08:51.292
Do the weights add up to one hundred percent?

110
00:08:51.342 --> 00:08:54.626
Are the counts plausible compared with the last delivery?

111
00:08:54.676 --> 00:08:59.891
The two subfunds and the two single funds of the month-end file all reconcile to the cent.

112
00:08:59.941 --> 00:09:05.917
The data-quality checks of FreeXmlToolkit do the same on the file; the loader repeats them on the data.

113
00:09:07.267 --> 00:09:08.886
The loader also checks codes.

114
00:09:08.936 --> 00:09:15.507
Every listed code, such as a price nature or a document type, is compared with the code list of the schema version.

115
00:09:15.557 --> 00:09:19.882
A code from a newer version than the sender declares is kept with a warning.

116
00:09:19.932 --> 00:09:25.638
Any other unknown code is an error, because it usually means a mapping problem on the sender's side.

117
00:09:25.688 --> 00:09:30.186
Findings are stored with the delivery, and a delivery with errors is held back.

118
00:09:31.536 --> 00:09:35.369
When a delivery fails, the sender needs a report it can act on.

119
00:09:35.419 --> 00:09:42.580
It names the file and its document ID, the rule that failed, where in the file, what was found and what was expected.

120
00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:47.325
And it says what happens now: the file was rejected, or loaded with warnings.

121
00:09:47.375 --> 00:09:51.763
A precise report gets a correction back in minutes, a vague one in days.

122
00:09:53.113 --> 00:09:56.106
Test the loader as carefully as the generator.

123
00:09:56.156 --> 00:10:00.340
Use realistic deliveries, with several funds, dates and natures.

124
00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:07.419
Add broken ones: a file cut off in the middle, an invalid file, a file from an unknown sender, a duplicate.

125
00:10:07.469 --> 00:10:11.521
Load the same files twice, then a correction, then a deletion.

126
00:10:11.571 --> 00:10:13.902
Load the generator's output back in.

127
00:10:13.952 --> 00:10:18.004
The example does all of this with one script, on every change.

128
00:10:19.354 --> 00:10:21.174
Only then is the data released.

129
00:10:21.224 --> 00:10:25.576
A status column on the delivery shows the way: loaded, checked, released.

130
00:10:25.626 --> 00:10:29.918
A function releases a checked delivery and records who did it, and when.

131
00:10:29.968 --> 00:10:33.236
Reports and downstream systems read only released data.

132
00:10:33.286 --> 00:10:39.568
Warnings go to the data team, who decide whether to release anyway or to ask the sender for a correction.

133
00:10:40.918 --> 00:10:50.764
Everything you saw in this film is in the complete example: the loader, the six deliveries and the four broken files, the queries, and the script that runs it all.

134
00:10:50.814 --> 00:10:58.170
You find it in the FundsXML examples repository on GitHub, in the folder PostgreSQL end to end, also as a single zip file.

135
00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:02.784
Start a database, run the script, and watch the same log on your own screen.

136
00:11:04.134 --> 00:11:12.763
That was loading FundsXML: validate first, never twice, streaming with one handler per repeating element, and reconcile before release.

137
00:11:12.813 --> 00:11:18.549
In the next film, you see the other direction: generating FundsXML from the same database.

138
00:11:18.599 --> 00:11:19.835
Thanks for watching.
